WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:05.442
The cutover is the planned switch from the old system to the new one: stop the old,

2
00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:07.974
move the last data, and start the new.

3
00:00:08.961 --> 00:00:12.873
It usually happens over a weekend, or during a planned shutdown.

4
00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:18.395
Its script is the cutover plan, hour by hour: every task with an owner,

5
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:21.113
a start time, a duration and a check.

6
00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:27.230
Often that is two hundred to four hundred lines, and nothing in it is improvised.

7
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.752
It starts with a freeze: from a fixed moment, here Friday at six in the evening,

8
00:00:33.926 --> 00:00:36.747
nobody enters anything new in the old system.

9
00:00:37.748 --> 00:00:42.078
First the last orders and goods movements, then a final stock count.

10
00:00:43.162 --> 00:00:45.589
If production carries on over the weekend,

11
00:00:45.752 --> 00:00:50.082
it is recorded on paper and entered into the new system afterwards.

12
00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.291
And weeks before, there is already a freeze on settings,

13
00:00:54.384 --> 00:00:57.844
so nobody changes the configuration at the last moment.

14
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:03.813
At fixed points there is a go/no-go: the decision to go ahead or stop,

15
00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:06.634
against criteria agreed in advance.

16
00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:12.526
For example: the reconciliation of stock and open orders is within tolerance,

17
00:01:12.724 --> 00:01:14.558
and the key interfaces work.

18
00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:19.591
There are no open blocking defects, and the key users are ready.

19
00:01:20.582 --> 00:01:24.680
A named person decides, usually the sponsor or plant manager.

20
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.374
Not the partner, who has an interest in going ahead.

21
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.255
The rollback point is the last moment you can

22
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.135
go back to the old system without losing work.

23
00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:38.460
Before it, a rollback plan is ready.

24
00:01:39.152 --> 00:01:43.865
After it, problems are fixed in the new system, because going back would cost more.

25
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.300
Place it so that a restore would still be finished well before the first shift.

26
00:01:50.365 --> 00:01:53.454
And rehearse the whole weekend once in a dress rehearsal:

27
00:01:53.535 --> 00:01:55.172
a full trial of the cutover,

28
00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:56.356
with a stopwatch.

29
00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:02.424
In a factory, Monday morning sets the clock: the first shift needs orders,

30
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.839
labels and the scales at six.

31
00:02:05.544 --> 00:02:07.111
Work back from that time.

32
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:10.903
What often goes wrong: there is no dress rehearsal,

33
00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:14.445
so the data load takes fourteen hours instead of nine.

34
00:02:15.454 --> 00:02:19.378
And the go/no-go turns into: we are too far in to stop now.

35
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.885
A sharp question: if the go/no-go on Saturday says no,

36
00:02:24.001 --> 00:02:25.777
what do the lines do on Monday?

37
00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:27.999
An example.

38
00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:33.259
A snack maker switches to a new ERP and MES over one weekend,

39
00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.546
with a plan timed in the dress rehearsal.

40
00:02:36.525 --> 00:02:41.308
Friday at six in the evening the freeze, the final stock count until nine,

41
00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:46.382
and from nine at night to six in the morning the migration of open orders and stock.

42
00:02:47.418 --> 00:02:50.831
The load took seven and a half hours in the dress rehearsal.

43
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.789
With a twenty percent margin, you plan nine hours.

44
00:02:55.823 --> 00:03:00.746
Saturday from six to noon the reconciliation, at noon the first go/no-go,

45
00:03:00.967 --> 00:03:05.819
and until eight in the evening interfaces on, test orders and test labels.

46
00:03:06.891 --> 00:03:11.454
Then until eight on Sunday morning the key users run the main scenarios,

47
00:03:11.651 --> 00:03:13.567
and the team fixes defects.

48
00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.545
At eight, the second go/no-go,

49
00:03:16.661 --> 00:03:20.759
also the rollback point: restoring the old system takes ten hours.

50
00:03:21.781 --> 00:03:25.728
On Monday at six, the first shift starts on the new system.

51
00:03:27.041 --> 00:03:31.581
Saturday, half past eleven: the stock difference is zero point two percent,

52
00:03:31.708 --> 00:03:35.017
within the agreed tolerance of zero point five percent.

53
00:03:36.052 --> 00:03:38.722
But the interface with the checkweighers fails.

54
00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:42.345
What do you advise at the first go/no-go?

55
00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:44.394
Go, with a condition.

56
00:03:45.395 --> 00:03:49.017
The checkweigher interface is only needed from Monday at six,

57
00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.430
so there is a window until Sunday at eight to fix it.

58
00:03:53.783 --> 00:03:55.838
What has to be true by Sunday at eight?

59
00:03:56.589 --> 00:04:00.664
The interface works, or a manual workaround has been tested.

60
00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:06.480
The workaround: operators enter the weights by hand, checked by a second person.

61
00:04:07.511 --> 00:04:09.183
If neither is true at eight,

62
00:04:09.368 --> 00:04:13.954
it is no-go: the old system is restored by about six on Sunday evening,

63
00:04:14.059 --> 00:04:15.998
well before Monday's first shift.

64
00:04:17.028 --> 00:04:20.151
The decision, with the reason, goes in the cutover log.

65
00:04:20.951 --> 00:04:25.165
And after Monday comes hypercare: the extra support after go-live.
